WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.250
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:11.590
Filigrees are detailed, thin patterns typically
found in jewelry, fabrics and ornaments, and

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:16.990
as you may imagine, crafting such motifs on
objects is incredibly laborious.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:22.419
This project is about leaving out the craftsmen
from the equation by choosing a set of target

00:00:22.419 --> 00:00:27.759
filigree patterns and creating a complex shape
out of them that can be easily 3D printed.

00:00:27.759 --> 00:00:34.140
The challenge lies in grouping and packing
up these patterns to fill a surface evenly.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:39.030
We start out with a base model with a poor
structure, which is not completely random,

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.250
but as you can see, is quite a forlorn effort.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:47.859
In several subsequent steps, we try to adjust
the positions and shapes of the filigree elements

00:00:47.859 --> 00:00:50.339
to achieve more pleasing results.

00:00:50.339 --> 00:00:55.839
The more pleasing results we define as one
that minimizes the amount of overlapping,

00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:59.019
and maximizes the connectivity of the final
shape.

00:00:59.019 --> 00:01:02.100
Sounds like an optimization problem from earlier.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.930
And, that is exactly what it is.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.430
Really cool, right?

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:11.770
The optimization procedure itself is far from
trivial, and the paper discusses possible

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:14.920
challenges and their solutions in detail.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:20.780
For instance, the fact that we can also add
control fields to describe our vision regarding

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:26.270
the size and orientation of the filigree patterns
is an additional burden that the optimizer

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.530
has to deal with.

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:32.450
We can also specify the ratio of the different
input filigree elements that we'd like to

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.020
see added to the model.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:39.030
The results are compared to previous work,
and the difference speaks for itself.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:44.409
However, it's important to point out that
even this thing that we call previous work

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:46.390
was still published this year.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.090
Talk about rapid progress in research!

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.490
Absolutely phenomenal work.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:57.090
The evaluation and the execution of the solution,
as described in the paper is also second to

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.090
none.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:01:59.399
Make sure to have a look.

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:05.070
And thank so much for taking the time to comment
on our earlier video about the complexity

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.100
of the series.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:12.660
I'd like to assure you we read every single
comment and found a ton of super helpful feedback

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:13.660
there.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:18.330
It seems to me that a vast majority of you
agree that a simple overlay text does the

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:23.530
job, and while it is there, it's even better
to make it clickable so it leads to a video

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:28.440
that explains the concept in a bit more detail
for the more curious minds out there.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.070
I'll try to make sure that everything is available
in mobile as well.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:37.200
You Fellow Scholars are the best and thanks
so much for everyone for leaving a comment.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.350
Also, please let me know in the comments section
if you have found this episode to be understandable

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:45.410
or if there were any terms that you've never
heard of.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:50.720
If everything was in order, that's also valuable
information, so make sure to leave a comment.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.800
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

